WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.299
If you caught my Direct Message last week,
you heard me explain why we shouldn't let

00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:13.450
politics make us crazy, yet the crazy seems
to be ramping up everywhere you look these

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:14.450
days.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:20.090
This constant onslaught of political bickering
has a very polarizing effect on most people:

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.020
The fighting either makes them obsessively
tune in to politics nonstop, or reflexively

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:30.280
tune out of politics altogether . With so
much news and commentary content out there,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:34.850
if you give me an hour or two of your week
to hear about political science, philosophy,

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:39.410
religion, or anything else we're discussing,
I consider the time you spend here with me

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.429
to be a pretty high honor.

00:00:41.429 --> 00:00:45.429
Those of you who follow me on social media
outside of YouTube probably saw I spent last

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:49.249
week on a farm with family and friends.

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:52.489
Though I spent most of the week hanging out
with 20 chickens and one duck who think he's

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:58.629
a chicken, I had a couple personal experiences
with actual real life humans which reminded

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:02.600
me why these aren't just abstract ideas we
talk about here.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.160
One of the people on the farm with us was
a trans woman in her 20's, who happened to

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.520
have grown up on a pot farm.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.750
She was also an avid hunter and gun rights
enthusiast who was painting her gun locker

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.750
bright blue and pink at the time of my visit.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:23.530
Yes, trans women are sometimes into guns,
but if you judge people as a collective instead

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:29.289
of as an individual, this is the type of person
who will always go ignored.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:35.090
I also spent time with a farmer who lived
almost 100% off his own land, but he was very

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:40.500
concerned with treating other people fairly
and making sure others had the same opportunity

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.329
to succeed which he had.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:47.130
While his day to day life had a very libertarian
bend to because he provided for himself and

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:52.170
lived on the land, there was no doubt he wanted
there to be assistance for people so they

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:57.649
could one day share in the same opportunity
and pride in work that he has attained.

00:01:57.649 --> 00:02:02.380
His live and let live attitude was blended
with his desire to good for others in his

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:03.689
community.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:09.360
He was a far cry from what some say about
those selfish libertarians, or what others

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.660
says about those authoritarian Leftys.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.920
While I was away, I also had a truly awe inspiring
hike up Mt.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.190
Rainier, which is part of the United States
National Park Service.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:24.810
This park is a great example of a natural
wonder we have in this country which is funded

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.270
and protected by the federal government.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:31.950
You know I'm a small government guy and would
prefer local and state funding, but a place

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:32.950
like Mt.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:37.990
Ranier, which is so gorgeous and powerful,
should have some federal funding, and I'd

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:43.350
say the same for Yellowstone National Park
or Yosemite.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:49.090
How much funding, or the best way to pay for
this funding, is the real question in the

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:50.090
debate.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.090
By the way, though, Mt.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.330
Ranier is also funded by state and local organizations,
and I met many great people along the trail

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:59.940
who were volunteering their time and energy
to a place they love so much.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.790
That's called putting your beliefs into action,
not just demanding the government pay for

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:09.240
something with other people's money, which
we talk about here often.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.680
My point is: people are complex, issues are
complex, and finding answers which will satisfy

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:19.100
everyone, always, is almost impossible.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:24.300
At the same time, not only do we all have
our own strengths and passions, but all also

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.150
have our own inconsistencies and blind spots.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:32.120
Our goal shouldn't be about purity tests so
that people fall in line or adhere to some

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.100
false notion of perfection.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.570
Our goal should be the acknowledgement of
our differences so we stand up for the freedom

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.830
and liberty that this country was founded
upon.

